      "filename": "The Stone of Anointing - The Church of the Holy Sepulchre (10805943173).jpg",
      "title": "File:The Stone of Anointing - The Church of the Holy Sepulchre (10805943173).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/The_Stone_of_Anointing_-_The_Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_%2810805943173%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Stone_of_Anointing_-_The_Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_(10805943173).jpg",
      "width": 4288,
      "height": 2848,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Jorge Láscar from Australia",
      "credit": "The Stone of Anointing - The Church of the Holy Sepulchre",
      "source": "The Stone of Anointing - The Church of the Holy Sepulchre (10805943173)",
      "description": "Just inside the entrance is The Stone of Anointing, also known as The Stone of Unction, which tradition claims to be the spot where Jesus' body was prepared for burial by Joseph of Arimathea. However, this tradition is only attested since the crusader era, and the present stone was only added in the 1810 reconstruction. The wall behind the stone was a temporary addition to support the arch above it, which had been weakened after the damage in the 1808 fire; the wall blocks the view of the rotunda, sits on top of the graves of four 12th-century kings, and is no longer structurally necessary. There is a difference of opinion as to whether it is the 13th Station of the Cross, which others identify as the lowering of Jesus from the cross and locate between the 11th and 12th station up on Calvary. The lamps that hang over the stone are contributed by Armenians, Copts, Greeks and Latins.\nThe Church of the Holy Sepulchre, also called the Basilica of the Holy Sepulchre, or the Church of the Resurrection by Eastern Christians, is a church within the Christian Quarter of the walled Old City of Jerusalem. It is a few steps away from the Muristan.\nThe site is venerated as Golgotha (the Hill of Calvary), where Jesus was crucified, and is said also to contain the place where Jesus was buried (the Sepulchre). The church has been a paramount – and for many Christians the most important – pilgrimage destination since at least the 4th century, as the purported site of the resurrection of Jesus. Today it also serves as the headquarters of the Greek Orthodox Patriarch of Jerusalem, while control of the building is shared between several Christian churches and secular entities in complicated arrangements essentially unchanged for centuries. Today, the church is home to branches of Eastern Orthodoxy and Oriental Orthodoxy as well as to Roman Catholicism. \n\nAnglican and Protestant Christians have no permanent presence in the Church – and some have regarded the alternative Garden Tomb, elsewhere in Jerusalem, as the true place of Jesus's crucifixion and resurrection [Wikipedia.org]",
      "dateCreated": "2012-09-05 06:23",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "church-of-the-holy-sepulchre-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "sv9h",
      "mediumPath": "_m/s/v/9/h/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre-wp/just-inside-entrance-s-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/s/v/9/h/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre-wp/just-inside-entrance-s-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Church of the Holy Sepulchre (10805156123).jpg",
      "title": "File:Church of the Holy Sepulchre (10805156123).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_%2810805156123%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_(10805156123).jpg",
      "width": 4288,
